BOARD MEETINGS
All JYO Team Managers are part of the JYO Board and are expected to attend the monthly JYO Board meetings during the season (September–March). The Team Manager is a conduit of information between the Board and the team's parents, and represents the team's parents in Board decisions — so it's important that you attend. If you can't make it, please ask the team coach or another team parent to attend in your place. If no one is able to attend, please review the meeting minutes within 24 hours, as there may be important topics to address.

Board meetings are held online via Zoom. Invitations will be sent via calendar invite, and agendas are generally sent at least a day before the meeting.

INSTRUCTIONAL TEAMS VS. LEAGUE TEAMS

Instructional Teams:
Instructional teams do not play league games. They practice once a week and play in various jamborees (a couple of exhibition games held over 1–2 days, hosted by an organization like JYO). The cost of jamborees is not included in the registration fee — see Tournaments and Jamborees below for the logistics.

Note: Instructional Team Managers are responsible for checking the "Tournament and Jamboree Tracker" document for jamborees, their deadlines, and applying accordingly. Please check regularly for registration dates — if one is missed, your team won't be able to attend that event.

Instructional Teams are comprised of these divisions: Co-Ed Kindergarten, Girls 1st Grade, Girls 2nd Grade, Girls 3rd Grade, Boys E Jr. 1st Grade, Boys E Jr. 2nd Grade, Boys E 3rd Grade.

League Teams:
League teams play 8 NYBA league games between December and February; the cost of league games is included in the player's registration fee. League teams also practice once a week, and there are game-day tasks the Team Manager needs to handle — see the Game Day Guide for instructions: https://docs.google.com/document/d/11b4gSPZExmVcoQr78kdi7JfqhSvQTmK1Q8-idn-1DEE/edit?usp=sharing

League Teams are comprised of these divisions: Girls G4 4th Grade, Girls G3 5th/6th Grade, Girls G2 7th/8th Grade, Boys D-American (DA) 4th/5th Grade, Boys D-National (DN) 6th Grade, Boys C-American (CA) 7th Grade, Boys C-National (CN) 8th/9th Grade.

PRE-SEASON TEAM BONDING
One of the best ways to start the season is to help players and families get to know one another before the season begins. Each team should organize a simple pre-season team-building event within the first few weeks of the season. The Team Manager can take the lead, or ask another team parent to help.

Some ideas include: playground meet-up, team picnic, park play date, team scavenger hunt, gathering before or after the first practice.

The goal isn't fundraising or basketball instruction — it's simply to help players build friendships and families connect with one another early in the season, and to give the coach a chance to introduce themselves and share goals and expectations for the season. For younger teams especially, strong relationships off the court often lead to a more enjoyable experience on the court.

Note: This event is not funded by JYO, and any costs should be split among the team's parents.

ATTENDANCE
To encourage commitment, ensure fairness, and support player development, practice attendance is tracked throughout the season. Attendance is automatically tracked by TeamSnap entries, but the Team Manager should still monitor it. If attendance looks low for any participant, please remind the family of our policies.

Attendance Tracking Period:
- September and October are exempt from attendance requirements
- Attendance tracking begins November 1
- All practices from November 1 through the final team activity of the season are included in attendance calculations

Attendance Requirement: Players are expected to attend at least 75% of scheduled team practices during the tracking period.

How late is considered absent from practice? Any attendance counts — as long as the athlete attends, it's considered attended.

Are there excused absences? All absences are counted the same (illness, family emergency, school activity, religious observance, or any other conflict).

What if the majority of the team can't make a practice? Practice should still happen if there are participants — please confirm with the coach. If you do decide to cancel practice:
- Update the schedule in TeamSnap to show practice is canceled, and message the team
- Update the "Available Gym Time" tab in the Master JYO Practice Schedule and add a comment tagging commission@sanmateojyo.org so another team may be able to use the gym

UNIFORMS AND GEAR
All JYO players need a uniform. Returning players should reuse their uniforms (assuming they still fit). For players who need a new uniform (new to the league, or needing a new size), the JYO Board/Commission will provide a way to order one. Note that uniform cost is not part of the registration fee — Team Managers will need to take orders and collect money, as in previous years, and should also keep track of players' jersey numbers. The JYO Board/Commission will arbitrate any decisions regarding jersey numbers.

Uniform Order Process — review the Uniform Order link (https://docs.google.com/spreadsheets/d/1NOGr6evJ4WpGLn1uWfICKOYGi73cB_YMY4XU-7T41RY/edit?gid=1722350148#gid=1722350148), provided by the TMSL, created by the registrar/uniform coordinator:
1. Review your team and make sure all players are accounted for
2. Email your team to remind them to order a uniform if they don't already have one on hand
3. Do not share the entire uniform file — it's for Team Managers only. You may want to track your team separately and enter directly for your team on the file
4. Update sizes accordingly. Don't edit other teams' information, to maintain data integrity
5. Team Managers are in charge of collecting payments from players (instructions will be provided in an email from the TMSL) — a check to the JYO Treasurer (made out to "San Mateo JYO") or Zelle may be the easiest way to track payments. Reach out to the TMSL for the Zelle QR code
6. Please submit before the designated due date — once the file is closed, we won't be able to reorder

Note: Jersey number availability varies by grade and is assigned by the uniform coordinator. Please reach out to the uniform coordinator if there are conflicts or requests for specific numbers — there are no guarantees requests will be granted. See the JYO Uniform Numbering System Proposal for more details on jersey number guidelines: https://docs.google.com/document/d/14tIE0F3L66oPmxX7lrrvHpqlhS9FeSzhcyVH5m1gx4I/edit?tab=t.0

For gently used uniforms, see the JYO Marketplace – Uniforms to Gift: https://docs.google.com/spreadsheets/d/1UK3RK8htWEhV1PtDh_PGtrwc3hFdIPq8hao6xcUQzvU/edit?usp=sharing. This platform serves as a hub for JYO families to connect and exchange uniforms; the Google Sheet isn't directly supervised by the uniform coordinator, but it facilitates communication among families.

The uniform coordinator has made a significant effort to ensure no players have duplicate jersey numbers within a team. Please don't alter jersey numbers — any changes may require the coordinator's approval. We'll try to accommodate requests for jersey transfers between siblings, but we can't guarantee they'll be fulfilled. For inquiries, please direct questions to the Uniform Coordinator.

PARENT SUPPORT
Your key to success is enlisting the help of your team's parents. The more you delegate, the easier it will be — and it also gets more people involved in the JYO community. It's highly suggested to organize a pre-season team bonding event to help get the team together at the beginning of the season, so new parents and players can get to know one another. End-of-season parties are also held to celebrate the season, give gifts to coaches if the team decides to (team-funded), and recognize the players. All roles you designate count toward the 6-hour volunteer requirement per family, per player (see Volunteer Hours Tracking below).

Example roles a team can allocate to families: pre-season team get-together coordinator, photo coordinator (practices, games, and/or team events for the end-of-year yearbook), team treasurer, jamboree/games event coordinator (meals), snack coordinator (not required, at the team's discretion), fundraising (not required), end-of-year team get-together coordinator, team gear (outside of uniforms, at the team's discretion, not supported by the JYO budget).

VOLUNTEER HOURS TRACKING
The board created a volunteer hours tracking sheet to understand how families are contributing to the JYO community. Families should log their 2026–2027 volunteer hours via the JYO Ad-Hoc Volunteer Hours 2026–2027 form (https://docs.google.com/forms/d/e/1FAIpQLSfD4BztTH9ZXhQPbc-j_GPrKN12OKsFr_6wHJ3z5eFqOPfHaw/viewform), and can check their running total on the JYO Volunteer Hours Family Look Up sheet (https://docs.google.com/spreadsheets/d/1sXVbB_aB1SWqD_BBB0j5Z2ew7XxLxJfKaJrBbSxtsB8/edit?gid=1234856404#gid=1234856404).

Our program relies on parent and family volunteers to provide a positive experience for all players. To ensure responsibilities are shared fairly, each family is expected to complete a minimum of six (6) volunteer hours per season. Hours may be earned starting with preseason activities through the final team event of the season, and can be accumulated across multiple activities, for example:

Game Day Support: scorebook/scoreboard operation, timekeeper, gym setup and cleanup
Team Support (see Parent Support above): team parent duties, team party planning, photo coordination, uniform distribution, snack coordination
JYO Jamboree Support: jamboree support, check-in tables, event setup and teardown

FUNDRAISING
Teams are welcome to organize their own fundraisers — this can be discussed at the pre-season team bonding event. Note: this isn't a requirement; it should be a collective team decision. The JYO name and logo may be used, but please identify the fundraiser as a team event rather than a JYO organization-wide one.

Before moving forward:
- Discuss the fundraising idea with your team/coach
- Notify the Team Manager Support Lead (TMSL) of the proposed fundraiser
- Allow time for the idea to be reviewed by the VP or Treasurer before moving forward
- Do not launch or collect money for a fundraiser until the review is complete

Collecting fundraising money: The Team Manager is responsible for coordinating how the team collects and tracks its fundraising money — the Team Manager must collect and manage the funds, or designate a parent on the team to help. Use the team's own payment collection method (for example, a team member's Venmo account). Do not use JYO's payment collection account for team fundraising proceeds.

