COLLECTING MONEY AND SIGNATURES
Don't rely solely on TeamSnap or email to get responses from parents. You'll generally see most parents at practices and games, so use that time to get player confirmations, money, and signatures for tournament waivers. Send an email before the practice/game letting parents know you'll collect these items in person, and bring your checklists and to-do lists with you.

You can choose to pay upfront for jamboree fees and collect money from families afterward, or request a set amount from each family upfront and deduct from it for each jamboree, team bonding event, or coach's gift. Collecting money upfront reduces how often you need to chase families for payment, and reduces how much you need to front yourself — coordinate based on what works best for you. Sample tracking sheet: https://docs.google.com/spreadsheets/d/1EfBBfOJ_xkUtYpqgvSkuOljZDrjekwWcfNBlj15fm6o/edit?usp=sharing

LEAGUE GAMES TO-DO'S — LEAGUE TEAMS ONLY
For all league games, you'll need to:
- Make sure you have a scorekeeper (scorebooks will be provided)
- Pay the referee fees (more details below)
- Provide the other team with a list of your players and jersey numbers playing that day (bring about 8 copies to hand out — you can keep it with the scorebook)
- Report the final score to the NYBA (the link will be provided at the start of league games)

For home games, you'll additionally need to:
- Have a clock/scoreboard operator (TBD if JYO will offer scoreboard training)
- Confirm the other team has provided their portion of the referee fees before the game starts — if not, you'll need to cover their portion and notify the JYO Board

Some other helpful hints:
- Keep instructions for running the scoreboard and a copy of the NYBA game rules at the scorekeepers' table
- Try to get veteran scorekeepers or scoreboard operators to train others. If your team has no veterans, reach out to the Athletic Commission or Board for training help

SCOREKEEPING — LEAGUE TEAMS ONLY
Make sure you have a scorebook — if not, contact the Athletic Commission at commission@sanmateojyo.org. In the week before each game, recruit a parent volunteer to keep score (it's nice to have two). In general, you're tracking fouls and points for each player — consult the coach if there are other stats they'd like tracked.

Before the game, the scorekeeper should get the roster from the opposing team's scorekeeper. It's good practice to compare points and fouls after each quarter.

Report the final score to the NYBA the same evening, via a Google Sheet set up by the NYBA — the commission will send a link when the season starts.

PAYING REFEREES
At the start of the season, the Athletic Director or Commissioners give the Treasurer a list of Team Managers' contacts and the amount to pay referees. The Treasurer then pays each Team Manager a lump sum via Venmo for the season's referee fees. Contact treasurer@sanmateojyo.org if you haven't received your team's league referee stipend by the start of the first league game.

All league teams have two referees per game. Each team playing needs to cover the payment for one referee, regardless of whether you're home or away. Leave an envelope with cash for your JYO share of the referee payment at the scorer's table, labeled "San Mateo JYO <division-team>" along with the game time — for example, "San Mateo JYO DA-White, 5:00 PM."

Check that the scorer's table has both teams' referee payments before the game starts — the referee should never have to ask for payment. If you don't see the other team's referee payment, remind their manager or coach.

If only one referee shows up for a game, that person is supposed to receive 1.5 times the normal fee. However, if they insist on the full fee for two referees, don't argue — just let them take both envelopes, and notify the JYO Board.

GYMS, GAMES, PRACTICES
For the first game in our home gym, the entire team needs to arrive 30 minutes before tip-off to help set up. The first team needs to make sure everything is ready by game time, including: chairs for both teams, bleachers pulled out, score table and scoreboard machine set up. This is generally handled by the gym facilities janitor, but it's good to be prepared. The last team should make sure the gym is clean — this includes picking up all empty water bottles and any garbage in the bleachers or gym.

Note: Gyms are difficult to rent, and we need to do our part to protect our ability to rent them.

TEAM PICTURES
Try to designate a team photographer or photo coordinator. It's always great to capture the season in pictures — have families upload their photos to your team's folder in the JYO 2026–2027 Team Photos folder: https://drive.google.com/drive/folders/1To_gu2pTwP-yx_nMz7unMnqVx6rbLOW1?usp=sharing. JYO publishes a yearbook every year, and we ask each team to submit group and individual photos.

Some helpful hints:
- Take a team photo with players in uniform, including the coaches — pick a practice day for everyone to wear their uniforms
- Let coaches and parents know ahead of time that you plan to take a photo so they're prepared
- Other good times for a group photo: after the game (once the coach finishes their post-game talk), between games at a tournament, at a scrimmage where everyone's in uniform, or at any other team event
- Try to get game-time shots of each player

QUESTIONS & DISPUTES FROM PARENTS — WHO TO SPEAK TO
If a parent has a question or concern about the team, they should speak to the Team Manager first. The Team Manager will discuss the concern with the coach, and should reach out to the appropriate JYO Commissioner if support is needed handling an issue with a coach. In general, parents shouldn't go directly to coaches with concerns, and shouldn't make coaching or strategy decisions — coaches are volunteers, and the board/commission empowers them to make coaching decisions in the team's best interest.

If a parent can't reach a resolution with the Team Manager (or has an issue with the Team Manager), they may contact their respective boys' or girls' basketball Commissioner by emailing commission@sanmateojyo.org, and it will be directed to the right person.

If no resolution is reached after discussing with the Commissioner, the parent can contact the board at board@sanmateojyo.org.

CLOSING REMARKS
Thank you for volunteering your time, energy, and talents to serve as a Team Manager. We recognize that this role requires a significant commitment. From organizing schedules and communicating with families to supporting coaches and helping behind the scenes, much of what you do often goes unnoticed — but it's absolutely essential to the success of our program.

Because of your dedication, our coaches can focus on developing players, our families stay informed, and our athletes can enjoy a fun, organized, and positive basketball experience.

Please remember that you're never expected to do this alone. We're one organization, one volunteer team, and we're here to support one another throughout the season. If you have questions, need guidance, or simply want to bounce an idea off someone, don't hesitate to reach out to the TMSL.

On behalf of our Board, coaches, players, and families, thank you for giving your time to make this season possible. We truly appreciate your commitment and wish you and your team a fun, rewarding, and memorable season.

Together, we create an environment where every player can learn, grow, compete, and build lasting friendships. Thank you for being an important part of our basketball family.`
